You are now one step away from conquering all 23 Gang of Four design patterns.
The Visitor Pattern solves a massive architectural problem related to the Open/Closed Principle (The "O" in SOLID
principles). It is heavily used in compilers, file system analyzers, and reporting systems.

The Problem: The Constantly Changing Core Classes
Imagine you have a ShoppingCart containing different types of items: Electronics, Groceries, and Liquor.

The Beginner Way:
Your boss says, "We need to calculate the tax for each item."
The Junior Developer opens all three classes and adds a calculateTax() method.
Next week, the boss says, "We need to calculate shipping costs."
The Junior Developer opens all three classes again and adds a calculateShipping() method.
Next month, the boss says, "We need a method to export the item data to JSON."

Why a Senior rejects this:
Every time a new business requirement is added, you are modifying the core data classes! This violates the Open/Closed
Principle (Classes should be open for extension, but closed for modification). If you keep opening the Electronics
class to add random business logic, you will eventually break it.

The Senior Solution: The Visitor Pattern (Double Dispatch)
The Visitor Pattern says: "Lock down the data classes. Never add business logic to them again. Instead, put the business
logic in an external 'Visitor' class, and let the Visitor walk through the data classes to do its job."

We give every data class a single method called accept(Visitor).
When the Visitor arrives, the data class says, "Welcome! Here I am, run your logic on me."
If we want to add 50 new features (Taxes, Shipping, JSON Export), we just create 50 new Visitor classes. We never touch
the data classes again!